[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186115369 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0fb80f93-0987-58b5-ba12-42ac451dfbe6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0fb80f93-0987-58b5-ba12-42ac451dfbe6" :question/id #uuid "0fb80f93-0987-58b5-ba12-42ac451dfbe6" :question/math-academy-id "q-317323" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+6?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/01/0183b9256896263aadfb0d18ae0a88aa11152c6e7e7d5693771f2e5bac20ac0a.png)\n\n \n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/31/31c5930bbeca76437d8e7a642bf52da4814bc5afda681b7974b383d3b8762c21.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $6$ units up to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+6.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,5)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=6.$\n\n![](images/c4/c4092a81a26d34360480e2ee46e99a17fa740b398038f36de427f3ed839bc68b.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e9033da7-e2fe-5546-be78-e76b67621ec0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e9033da7-e2fe-5546-be78-e76b67621ec0" :answer-field/id #uuid "e9033da7-e2fe-5546-be78-e76b67621ec0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ef42d8b8-af1b-506e-8041-6901cf5ee8e9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dac4b509-3d83-5182-be7b-2caa46451772" "answer-ef42d8b8-af1b-506e-8041-6901cf5ee8e9" "answer-0153aeb0-58c4-5c73-8036-9df357871177" "answer-94c833a6-4aa0-58ca-9b4d-89b409187095" "answer-7c125b62-5ac5-55f9-9f39-b1d75b192a8f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dac4b509-3d83-5182-be7b-2caa46451772" :answer/id #uuid "dac4b509-3d83-5182-be7b-2caa46451772" :answer/type :answer.type/i